<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TPanel</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TPanel" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPanel</tt> is a presentational container for other controls. It renders a
<tt>&lt;div&gt;</tt> element holding its body content.
</p>

<h2>Laying out the content</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Five properties shape the panel. Each one writes a CSS declaration into the element's
style attribute rather than an HTML attribute, so the result behaves like any other
styling.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Values</th><th>Writes</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Wrap</tt></td>
	<td>true or false. Defaults to true.</td>
	<td><tt>white-space: nowrap</tt> when false.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>HorizontalAlign</tt></td>
	<td><tt>NotSet</tt>, <tt>Left</tt>, <tt>Center</tt>, <tt>Right</tt>, <tt>Justify</tt></td>
	<td><tt>text-align</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Direction</tt></td>
	<td><tt>NotSet</tt>, <tt>LeftToRight</tt>, <tt>RightToLeft</tt></td>
	<td><tt>direction</tt>, which suits a right to left language.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>ScrollBars</tt></td>
	<td><tt>None</tt>, <tt>Auto</tt>, <tt>Horizontal</tt>, <tt>Vertical</tt>, <tt>Both</tt></td>
	<td><tt>overflow</tt>, or one of its single-axis forms. A scrolling panel needs a <tt>Height</tt> or a <tt>Width</tt> for the overflow to have an effect.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>BackImageUrl</tt></td>
	<td>A URL</td>
	<td><tt>background-image</tt>.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>Grouping with a legend</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Setting <tt>GroupingText</tt> puts a <tt>&lt;fieldset&gt;</tt> inside the panel's
<tt>&lt;div&gt;</tt>, with the text as its <tt>&lt;legend&gt;</tt>. The body content then
sits inside that fieldset. A group of related form fields under one caption is the case
this serves.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TPanel GroupingText="Delivery address"&gt;
  &lt;com:TTextBox ID="Street" /&gt;
  &lt;com:TTextBox ID="City" /&gt;
&lt;/com:TPanel&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>The default button</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>DefaultButton</tt> takes the id of a button inside the panel. Pressing the return key
while the focus is within the panel then clicks that button. The panel looks the control
up in its own naming container, and raises a <tt>TInvalidDataValueException</tt> when the
id names no control at all. An id that names a control which is not a button is ignored,
and the return key then does nothing.
</p>

<h2>Shadow</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.0" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>Style.BoxShadow</tt> writes a <tt>box-shadow</tt> declaration. It takes the CSS value
as written.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TPanel Style.BoxShadow="2px 2px 4px #888" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
